WEBVTT

00:00:00.712 --> 00:00:04.545
How does rainfall become flow, and how does flow become

00:00:04.438 --> 00:00:09.032
flooding? Explore both questions in Rohan Creek, a watershed with

00:00:08.925 --> 00:00:14.045
one outlet. Begin with rainfall and infiltration. Explore terrain and

00:00:13.938 --> 00:00:19.158
land-use layers, or open additional storm and land-cover options. Follow

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:24.883
infiltration and runoff, then create a hydrograph. Hydrology gives discharge

00:00:24.763 --> 00:00:30.545
through time, not water height. Next, explore depth, water elevation,

00:00:30.775 --> 00:00:35.495
velocity, and flood extent. Replay the flood, inspect a point,

00:00:35.788 --> 00:00:40.445
or view the mesh. Change one input. Pin hydrographs, switch

00:00:40.338 --> 00:00:44.482
between original and changed floods, and read the comparison table.

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:49.182
Four numbered steps keep you on track. Optional explanations and

00:00:49.075 --> 00:00:53.332
equations add detail. These flood maps are illustrative. Can you

00:00:53.225 --> 00:00:57.408
connect rainfall, runoff, discharge, and inundation?
